.docname {Installation}
.include {docs}

## Download a release

Every tagged release on the .repolink {GitLab Releases page} {-/releases} has ready-to-run builds, so you don't need a Rust toolchain. Download the archive for your platform, unpack it, and run `gol-bevy`.

Each archive contains the same three files: the binary, the README, and the licence. The app needs nothing else on disk: built-in patterns and the UI font are embedded in the binary.

### Linux (x86_64)

Download the `x86_64-linux.tar.gz` archive. It needs:

- glibc 2.39 or newer (Ubuntu 24.04, Debian 13, Fedora 40, or later)
- a Wayland or X11 desktop

```shell
tar -xzf gol-bevy-<version>-x86_64-linux.tar.gz
./gol-bevy-<version>-x86_64-linux/gol-bevy
```

### Windows (x86_64)

Download the `x86_64-windows.zip` archive, unzip it, and run `gol-bevy.exe`. Release builds open no console window.

### macOS (universal)

The macOS archive runs natively on both Apple silicon and Intel Macs. It is attached to each release by hand, so it can appear a little after the Linux and Windows builds.

> Warning: The binary is not signed, so macOS blocks it the first time you open it.

Either allow it under **System Settings → Privacy & Security**, or clear the quarantine flag in the unpacked folder:

```shell
xattr -d com.apple.quarantine gol-bevy
```

## Run from source

If you have the repository checked out, run the app with Cargo:

```shell
cargo run
```

The first build takes a while, because dependencies are compiled with full optimization even in dev builds. See [Building from source](development.qd) for the pinned toolchain, the Linux system libraries, and faster rebuilds.

## Window and board size

The window opens at 1200 × 800 and can be resized down to 680 × 420. On first launch the board is sized to fill the window. After that, the board keeps the size you choose in [Settings](settings.qd), whatever the window size.

## Next steps

Continue with [First steps](first-steps.qd) to place your first pattern and run it.
